from helpers import danger, caution, tip, check, table, fig

def part1(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p1"><small>Part 1</small>Basics: Concrete, Mortar, Plaster &amp; Safety</h1>']
    h.append('<h2 id="what">1.1 What concrete, mortar and plaster are</h2>')
    h.append('<p>All three are the same idea: a <b>binder</b> (a powder that reacts and hardens) glues together a skeleton of <b>aggregate</b> (sand and stone), with <b>water</b> to start the reaction and make the mix workable. What changes is the size of the aggregate and the job.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Material", "Recipe (typical, by volume)", "Job", "Notes"], [
        ["<b>Concrete</b>", "Cement + sand + gravel, 1:2:3 to 1:3:6", "Footings, slabs, walls, well rings, cisterns, posts", "Strong in compression, weak in tension (needs steel for beams, lintels, slabs that span)"],
        ["<b>Mortar</b>", "Cement and/or lime + sand, 1:3 to 1:1:6", "Glue between brick, block and stone", "Should be <i>weaker</i> than the units it joins so cracks run through joints, not bricks"],
        ["<b>Plaster / render / stucco</b>", "Lime or cement (+ fiber) + fine sand, 1:2 to 1:3", "Protective skin on walls, cistern linings", "Lime plaster breathes (good on earth walls); cement render is harder and waterproof"],
        ["<b>Grout</b>", "Cement + sand (+ pea gravel), soupy", "Fills block cores around rebar", "Pourable; still keep water as low as possible"],
        ["<b>Ferrocement</b>", "Rich mortar 1:2–1:3 on layers of wire mesh", "Thin tanks, roofs, boats", "1–2 in (25–50 mm) thick, very strong if well cured"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="roles">1.2 The three ingredients and what each does</h2>')
    h.append('<div class="cols2"><div><h3>Binder</h3><p><b>Portland cement</b> reacts with water (hydration) and hardens even under water. It reaches about 70% of its strength in 7 days and full design strength at 28 days, then keeps gaining slowly for years if kept damp. <b>Lime</b> (slaked lime) hardens slowly by absorbing CO₂ from air (carbonation) — it needs air, not water, and is soft, flexible and self-healing. <b>Hydraulic lime</b>, <b>natural cement</b> and <b>lime + pozzolan</b> sit in between: they set with water like a weak cement. <b>Gypsum</b> sets in minutes but dissolves in rain — indoor use only.</p></div>'
             '<div><h3>Aggregate</h3><p>Aggregate is 60–75% of concrete’s volume. It should be <b>hard, clean and well graded</b> — a mix of sizes so small particles fill the gaps between big ones and less paste is needed. Sand = particles under ¼ in (5 mm); gravel = ¼–1½ in (5–38 mm). Angular crushed stone bonds better than round river gravel but is harder to work. Dirt, clay coatings, leaves, salt and sugar all weaken concrete.</p>'
             '<h3>Water</h3><p>Water starts the reaction and makes the mix flow. <b>Only about a quarter of the cement’s weight in water is needed chemically</b>; anything extra is there for workability and leaves pores when it dries. <b>Too much water is the #1 cause of weak homemade concrete.</b></p></div></div>')
    h.append('<h2 id="ratios">1.3 Mix ratios and the water–cement ratio</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Ratios are written <b>cement : sand : gravel</b> by loose volume. Use the same bucket for everything (a “gauge box” or a 5-gal pail). A 94-lb US bag of portland cement is 1 ft³ (28 L); 40 kg is about 0.9 ft³ (26 L). Because sand fills the gaps between stones, 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 buckets of dry ingredients makes only about 4 buckets of concrete.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.mix_ratios(), "<b>Figure 1. Common mixes and the effect of water.</b> Bag counts are per cubic yard (0.76 m³). Lower water–cement ratio = stronger, less permeable, more durable concrete — but it must still be workable enough to compact fully.", "max-width:83%"))
    h.append(table(["Use", "Mix (C:S:G)", "Max gravel", "Water per 94-lb bag", "Slump (sag of a cone)"], [
        ["Water tanks, well rings, lintels, beams, posts", "1 : 2 : 3", "¾ in (20 mm); ½ in for thin rings", "5–5½ gal (19–21 L)", "2–4 in (50–100 mm)"],
        ["Footings, slabs, walls, foundations", "1 : 2 : 4", "1 in (25 mm)", "5½–6 gal (21–23 L)", "3–4 in"],
        ["Mass fill, levelling pads, unreinforced footings", "1 : 3 : 6", "1½ in (38 mm)", "6–6½ gal", "3–5 in"],
        ["Hollow blocks (very dry, tamped)", "1 : 6–8 (sand / fine gravel)", "⅜ in (10 mm)", "just enough to hold a squeezed ball", "zero"]], numcols=()))
    h.append(tip("Field slump test", "Fill a bottomless cone (a 12 in tall cone 4 in wide at top, 8 in at bottom; or a cut-down plastic bucket) in three layers, rod each layer 25 times, lift the cone straight up and measure how far the concrete sags. 1–2 in = stiff; 3–4 in = normal for hand placing; 6+ in = too wet — add a little cement and sand/gravel in the original ratio, never just more water."))
    h.append('<h2 id="strength">1.4 What strength to expect</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Material", "Typical compressive strength", "Good for"], [
        ["Portland-cement concrete, well made and cured", "2,500–4,000 psi (17–28 MPa)", "Everything in this guide"],
        ["Same concrete, too wet / air-dried", "often 1,000–2,000 psi (7–14 MPa)", "Non-structural only"],
        ["Cement-lime mortar (Type N 1:1:6 / Type S 1:½:4½)", "750 / 1,800 psi (5 / 12 MPa) minimum (ASTM C270)", "Brick, block, stone walls"],
        ["Hydraulic lime mortar (NHL 3.5–5)", "~300–700 psi (2–5 MPa)", "Masonry, damp locations"],
        ["Lime putty mortar (air lime)", "~70–300 psi (0.5–2 MPa), slowly rising", "Soft brick, stone, earth"],
        ["Lime–pozzolan concrete", "~300–1,500 psi (2–10 MPa) after months", "Footings, floors, cisterns (thick)"],
        ["Fired clay brick", "1,500–10,000+ psi (10–70 MPa)", "Walls, kilns, ovens"],
        ["Adobe / CEB / rammed earth (unstabilized)", "~300 psi (2 MPa); NM code minimum 300 psi", "Thick walls kept dry"],
        ["Stabilized CEB / rammed earth (5–10% cement)", "~400–1,000+ psi (3–7+ MPa)", "Walls, some damp exposure"]]))
    h.append('<p class="small">Values compiled from FM 5-428/TM 3-34.44, ASTM C270 via NPS Preservation Brief 2, Auroville Earth Institute, and the New Mexico Earthen Building Materials Code. Ranges are indicative.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="safety">1.5 Safety: burns, dust, lifting, fire and gas</h2>')
    h.append(danger("Wet cement and lime are caustic — they burn skin and blind eyes", "Fresh concrete has a pH of 12–13. <b>Cement burns often don’t hurt until hours later</b>, and can go deep enough to need skin grafts — kneeling in wet concrete or letting it soak through boots and gloves causes most of them. Wear alkali-resistant (nitrile/butyl) gloves, long sleeves, waterproof boots with trousers <i>over</i> the tops, and goggles. Wash skin promptly with clean water (a little vinegar is not a substitute for long rinsing). <b>Splash in the eye: flush with clean water for at least 15 minutes</b>, holding the lid open, then get medical help. Quicklime is far worse than cement — see Part 3."))
    h.append(danger("Silica dust: cutting, crushing and sweeping", "Cutting or crushing concrete, block, brick and stone releases respirable crystalline silica, which causes silicosis (permanent lung scarring) and lung cancer. <b>Work wet</b>: keep a hose or sprayer on the cutting point, crush rubble damp, and clean up wet. Wear at least an N95 (better: P100 half-mask) when crushing rubble, sweeping, or opening/emptying bags of cement and lime. Never dry-sweep cement dust indoors."))
    h.append(caution("Lifting and heavy work", "A 94-lb bag of cement, a 5-gal bucket of wet concrete (~100 lb / 45 kg), a concrete block (35–40 lb) and a 1 m well ring (~300 kg / 660 lb) are serious loads. Lift with legs, keep loads close, share heavy lifts, use levers, rollers, ramps, tripods and chain hoists. Never work under a suspended ring or in an unshored trench deeper than about 4 ft (1.2 m) — trench walls collapse without warning."))
    h.append(danger("Kilns: fire, burns and carbon monoxide", "Lime and brick kilns run at 900–1,100 °C for days. They produce <b>carbon monoxide</b> (odorless, deadly) and CO₂ (lime burning releases large amounts — it pools in low spots, pits and cellars). Site kilns outdoors, away from buildings and dry grass, downwind of camp; never sleep near a burning kiln or in a hollow below it; never climb onto a kiln top. Keep water, shovels and dirt for fire control. Symptoms of CO: headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion — move to fresh air immediately."))
    return "\n".join(h)

def part2(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p2"><small>Part 2</small>Finding &amp; Salvaging Materials</h1>']
    h.append('<p>In the first months after a collapse, the fastest route to concrete is <b>salvage</b>: bagged cement and lime, stockpiled sand and gravel, rebar and mesh, concrete block and brick. Most of it will be sitting in places nobody else thinks to look. Make-it-yourself binders (Part 3) are the long-term plan once salvage runs out.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="cement">2.1 Bagged cement and lime</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Where to look", "What you’ll find", "Notes"], [
        ["Hardware stores, lumber yards, home centers", "Portland cement (Type I/II), premixed concrete and mortar mix, masonry cement, hydraulic (plug) cement, hydrated lime", "Premix 80-lb bags are only ~12–15% cement — fine for small jobs, poor value for large ones"],
        ["Farm and ranch supply, feed stores", "Hydrated lime, barn lime, quicklime (sometimes), fence-post concrete, ag lime", "<b>Ag lime / garden lime is ground limestone (CaCO₃) — it is NOT a binder.</b> Read the bag: you want “hydrated lime” or Ca(OH)₂"],
        ["Construction sites, block plants, ready-mix plants, precast yards", "Pallets of cement, cement silos, sand and gravel stockpiles, rebar, mesh, forms, block, pipe and culvert sections", "Silo cement may have set at the edges; scoop from the center"],
        ["Masonry and landscape suppliers", "Type S lime, mortar, brick, pavers, stone, washed sand and gravel", "Often the best stocks of lime"],
        ["Pool supply, water treatment plants, paper mills", "Hydrated lime, quicklime (water treatment)", "Treat quicklime with full safety gear"],
        ["Power plants (coal), steel mills", "Fly ash (pozzolan — can replace 15–25% of cement), slag", "Class F fly ash works best with lime or cement"]]))
    h.append('<h3>Storage and shelf life</h3><p>Cement doesn’t expire, but it absorbs moisture from air and slowly hydrates in the bag. Kept dry, sealed in plastic, on pallets off concrete floors and away from walls, bags stay usable for a year or more; in a damp shed, paper bags may go lumpy within 1–3 months. Stack bags close together (no more than ~10 high), cover with tarps, use oldest first. Lime keeps better than cement; quicklime must be kept bone-dry in sealed steel drums — <b>never in a wooden building</b>, because wet quicklime gets hot enough to ignite wood, straw and paper.</p>')
    h.append(check("Checking old cement before you trust it", [
        "Roll and knead the bag: packed but powdery cement (“warehouse set”) loosens and is fine",
        "Lumps that crush easily between your fingers: OK — break them up and use",
        "Hard lumps you can’t crush: screen them out through ¼ in mesh; use the powder for non-structural work or at 10–20% more cement per batch",
        "Mostly hard or a solid brick: it has hydrated — crush and use only as aggregate",
        "Test batch: make a fist-size pat, keep it damp; it should be firm in 1 day and hard (can’t scratch with a fingernail) in 3–7 days"]))
    h.append('<h2 id="sand">2.2 Sand and gravel</h2>')
    h.append('<div class="cols2"><div><h3>Where to find it</h3><ul>'
             '<li><b>Gravel pits and quarries</b> — every county has them; stockpiles are already graded and often washed.</li>'
             '<li><b>Riverbeds and bars</b> — inside bends and below rapids hold clean, well-sorted sand and gravel. Dig from the active channel edge; avoid silty backwaters.</li>'
             '<li><b>Glacial deposits</b> (Canada, northern US, mountains) — eskers (long winding ridges), kames, outwash plains and terraces are natural gravel sorting machines.</li>'
             '<li><b>Alluvial fans and dry washes</b> (arroyos) in deserts — coarse, angular, well graded.</li>'
             '<li><b>Road and rail ballast</b>, landscape yards, playgrounds, sand-bag stockpiles, pipe bedding.</li>'
             '<li><b>Crushed rubble</b> — see 2.3.</li></ul></div>'
             '<div><h3>What to avoid</h3><ul>'
             '<li><b>Beach and sea-dredged sand</b>: salt corrodes steel. Use only after washing with fresh water until the rinse water no longer tastes salty (3–5 rinses). Beach sand is also often very uniform and round — mix it with coarser sand.</li>'
             '<li><b>Desert dune sand</b>: wind-rounded and too fine on its own; blend with coarse sand.</li>'
             '<li><b>Topsoil, clay-coated or silty sand</b>: organic matter retards setting; clay and silt weaken bond. Use the jar test.</li>'
             '<li><b>Soft or flaky stone</b>: shale, mudstone, chalk, mica schist, coal, wood — they break down.</li>'
             '<li><b>Gypsum sand</b> (e.g., White Sands, NM) and anything from mine tailings: sulfates attack concrete.</li></ul></div></div>')
    h.append(fig(D.jar_test(), "<b>Figure 2. Jar tests.</b> A — what a soil or pit sample is made of (also used for adobe and rammed earth in Part 5). B — a quick check of concrete sand cleanliness (after FM 5-428 field method and extension soil-texture tests).", "max-width:77%"))
    h.append(tip("Washing sand by hand", "Shovel sand into a sloped trough, wheelbarrow or barrel with holes near the top; run water through and stir so the muddy water overflows and carries silt away. Or tie sand in a burlap/feed sack and slosh it in a stream. Three washes usually fix silty sand. Organic check: shake sand with a 3% lye (sodium hydroxide) solution and leave 24 h — liquid darker than weak tea means too much organic matter (field version of ASTM C40)."))
    h.append('<h2 id="rca">2.3 Recycled concrete, brick and block rubble</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Broken concrete is excellent coarse aggregate. FHWA has approved crushed concrete for new pavement for decades. Break slabs with a sledgehammer or by dropping them, then crush to 1 in (25 mm) and smaller with a sledge on a steel plate or a jaw made from two heavy plates. Pull out steel (save it), wood, plastic, asphalt and especially <b>drywall/plaster (gypsum)</b>, which causes sulfate attack.</p>')
    h.append('<ul><li><b>Pre-wet</b> recycled aggregate — old mortar soaks up water and will dry out your mix.</li><li><b>Screen out the fines</b> (below ¼ in) and use natural sand instead; crushed-concrete fines need much more water.</li><li>Expect 10–25% less strength than with natural gravel; use 1:2:3 instead of 1:2:4 for the same job.</li><li><b>Crushed fired brick</b> makes decent aggregate for non-structural concrete, and finely ground brick dust is a <b>pozzolan</b> (Part 3).</li><li>Intact salvaged brick, block and stone are worth more whole — knock off the old mortar with a brick hammer. Old soft lime mortar cleans off easily; hard cement mortar may not.</li></ul>')
    h.append(danger("Wet down rubble before breaking it", "Crushing concrete and brick is one of the highest silica-dust jobs there is. Keep the pile wet, work upwind, and wear a P100 or N95 respirator and eye protection — chips fly like shrapnel."))
    h.append('<h2 id="steel">2.4 Rebar, mesh and other reinforcement</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Concrete is roughly 10× weaker in tension than in compression. Anything that bends — lintels, beams, slabs, tank walls, posts, well caps — needs steel on the side that stretches. Sources: lumber yards and construction sites (rebar #3–#5, welded wire mesh), demolition (bars from broken slabs, straighten cold), fencing (high-tensile wire, chain-link, field fence, T-posts), farm stores (cattle panels — excellent for slabs and ferrocement), bed frames and angle iron, old pipe.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Rule", "Value"], [
        ["Bar sizes (US)", "#3 = ⅜ in (10 mm), #4 = ½ in (13 mm), #5 = ⅝ in (16 mm): number = eighths of an inch"],
        ["Concrete cover (minimum)", "3 in (75 mm) cast against earth · 1½–2 in (40–50 mm) exposed to weather · ¾ in (20 mm) indoors, dry · ferrocement ⅛–¼ in"],
        ["Lap splice", "≈ 40–60 bar diameters: #4 bar → overlap 24 in (600 mm), tied with wire"],
        ["Condition", "Light, tight rust is fine (it helps bond). Knock off flaky scale. Remove oil, grease, paint, mud"],
        ["Position", "Steel goes where concrete stretches: bottom of slabs, beams and lintels; near the outside of tank walls (hoops)"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="water">2.5 Water</h2>')
    h.append('<p>If you would drink it, you can mix concrete with it. Rainwater, well water and clear stream water are fine. Avoid water with sugar (even a little stops cement setting), oil, detergent, swamp/peat water, sewage, and mine or industrial runoff. <b>Never use seawater for reinforced concrete or anything with steel in it</b> — chlorides rust the steel, which swells and splits the concrete. In a pinch, seawater can be used for plain unreinforced mass concrete, but expect efflorescence and lower long-term strength. Curing needs as much water again as mixing: a cubic yard of concrete takes about 30–40 gal (115–150 L) to mix, plus water to keep it damp for a week.</p>')
    return "\n".join(h)

def part3(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p3"><small>Part 3</small>Making Binders From Scratch</h1>']
    h.append('<p>When bagged cement is gone, people go back to the binders humans used for 9,000 years: <b>lime</b>, <b>lime with pozzolan</b> (Roman concrete), <b>hydraulic lime and natural cement</b>, and <b>gypsum</b>. All start with a rock and a fire.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="limestone">3.1 Finding limestone and other lime sources</h2>')
    h.append(table(["Raw material", "How to recognize it", "Quality for lime"], [
        ["Limestone", "Gray, cream or tan; often has fossil shells; scratches easily with a steel knife but not a fingernail; fizzes in acid", "Pure (high-calcium) limestone → fat air lime; clayey limestone → hydraulic lime"],
        ["Marble", "Crystalline, sugary sparkle, white to gray; same hardness and fizz", "Excellent, very pure"],
        ["Chalk", "Soft, white, marks paper", "Excellent, burns easily, crumbly in kiln"],
        ["Shells (oyster, clam, mussel, conch), coral", "Middens, beaches, shell banks, oyster-processing piles", "Excellent; wash off salt and sand first. Never harvest living coral reefs"],
        ["Caliche / calcrete", "Hard white crust a few feet down in dry-climate soils", "Usable; often sandy — gives weaker, slightly hydraulic lime"],
        ["Travertine, tufa, marl", "Spongy or layered spring deposits; marl is limy clay", "Travertine good; marl → hydraulic lime or natural cement"],
        ["Dolomite", "Looks like limestone; fizzes only when powdered", "Magnesian lime: usable, slakes slower; burn a bit cooler"]]))
    h.append(tip("The vinegar (acid) test", "Scrape fresh rock into powder with a knife or a file, put it in a cup and add vinegar (or a few drops of muriatic acid if you have it — far stronger). <b>Steady fizzing = calcium carbonate.</b> Household vinegar reacts slowly: watch closely and listen. Powdered dolomite fizzes weakly; quartz, granite and basalt don’t fizz at all and are too hard to scratch with steel. Gypsum is softer (scratches with a fingernail) and doesn’t fizz."))
    h.append('<h2 id="kiln">3.2 Burning lime: a simple kiln</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Heat drives CO₂ out of calcium carbonate. The reaction needs about <b>900 °C (1,650 °F)</b> at the center of every stone, held for many hours; real kilns run 900–1,100 °C. Underburnt stone has a hard core that won’t slake; overburnt (“dead-burnt”) lime slakes slowly and poorly. About <b>1.75–1.8 kg of limestone makes 1 kg of quicklime</b> — 44% of the weight leaves as CO₂.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.lime_kiln(), "<b>Figure 3. A simple intermittent (flare) lime kiln, cross-section.</b> Built of stone into a bank so the earth insulates it and you can load from above. Variants: a pit kiln, or a “clamp” of alternate layers of wood and stone covered with earth for small batches.", "max-width:79%"))
    h.append('<h3>Building and firing</h3><ol>'
             '<li><b>Site</b>: a bank or hillside near the stone and firewood, away from buildings and dry grass, with an open downwind side. Internal size for a family-scale kiln: 4–6 ft (1.2–1.8 m) across, 6–10 ft (2–3 m) tall.</li>'
             '<li><b>Walls</b>: dry-laid or clay-mortared stone, 1–2 ft thick; line the inside with fire brick, firebrick salvage, or clay plaster. Leave a stoke/draw arch at the bottom on the downhill side.</li>'
             '<li><b>Arch</b>: build a self-supporting arch of the largest limestone blocks over the fire space (or a grate of steel bars if you have them); fill above with stone broken to fist size (2–6 in / 5–15 cm), larger pieces low, smaller higher, leaving gaps for draft.</li>'
             '<li><b>Fire slowly</b> for the first 6–12 hours (steam and smoke — damp stone can burst), then hard for 2–4 days, feeding dry wood continuously. A 100 ft³ charge burns roughly its own weight in dry wood.</li>'
             '<li><b>Watch the color</b>: dull red (~600 °C) → cherry (~800 °C) → <b>bright orange (900–1,000 °C)</b> through the whole charge, including the top layer. Flames at the top become short and clear when the burn is nearly done; the charge settles noticeably (10–20%).</li>'
             '<li><b>Test</b>: rake out a sample from the top. Burnt lime is lighter, chalky, and doesn’t fizz in vinegar. Drop it in water: good lime cracks, hisses, steams and falls to powder within minutes. A hard core means burn longer.</li>'
             '<li><b>Cool</b> 1–2 days with the openings closed, then draw the lime out into dry steel containers. Slake it or seal it the same day.</li></ol>')
    h.append(danger("Quicklime is one of the most dangerous things in this guide", "Quicklime (CaO) reacts violently with water — including the water in your eyes, sweat and lungs — producing heat and a strong alkali. <b>A speck in the eye can cause permanent blindness.</b> Always wear sealed goggles (not just glasses), a respirator, gauntlet gloves, long sleeves and boots. Keep a large bucket of clean water (sugar water is often used in the trade as an eyewash; plain clean water is fine) right beside you for 15+ minute eye flushing. Never handle it barehanded or carry it in pockets, never store it near combustibles or in a wooden building, and keep children and animals far away. Slaking can boil and spit hot caustic paste."))
    h.append('<h2 id="slake">3.3 Slaking: quicklime to lime putty</h2>')
    h.append(fig(D.lime_cycle(), "<b>Figure 4. The lime cycle.</b> Burnt lime returns to limestone in the wall — which is why lime mortar is soft, breathable and slowly self-healing.", "max-width:74%"))
    h.append('<div class="cols2"><div><h3>Making lime putty (for mortar and plaster)</h3><ol>'
             '<li>Fill a steel tub or a lined pit about ⅓–½ with water.</li>'
             '<li><b>Add quicklime to water, never water to lime</b>, a shovelful at a time (roughly 1 part lime to 3 parts water by volume). Stand back: it will hiss, boil and steam.</li>'
             '<li>Hoe constantly so lumps don’t “burn” (overheat and form gritty lumps) or “drown” (cool too quickly). Add water if it gets stiff and dry-looking.</li>'
             '<li>When it stops boiling, run the cream through a ¼ in (6 mm) screen to remove unburnt cores.</li>'
             '<li>Store under a layer of water in a covered pit or barrel. Mature at least 2–4 weeks for mortar, 2–3 months or more for fine plaster — older is better; unslaked particles that pop later ruin plaster.</li></ol></div>'
             '<div><h3>Dry hydrate and hot-mixing</h3><p><b>Dry hydrate</b> (like bagged hydrated lime): sprinkle just enough water on quicklime lumps (about 1 part water to 3 parts lime by weight) so they fall to dry powder. Necessary for hydraulic limes, which would set in a putty pit.</p>'
             '<p><b>Hot-mixed mortar</b>: slake broken quicklime directly with damp sand (≈1 part lime : 3 parts sand), turn it over, let it cool, then knock it up with water before use. This is the historic method — MIT research (Seymour et al., 2023) shows Roman builders used it and that the leftover lime lumps let cracks self-heal. Expect temperatures of 100–300 °C in the heap: full protective gear.</p>'
             '<h3>Lime mortar and plaster recipes</h3><ul><li><b>Mortar</b>: 1 putty : 3 sharp, well-graded sand.</li><li><b>Base plaster</b>: 1 : 3, plus chopped hair, straw or sisal fibers.</li><li><b>Finish plaster</b>: 1 : 1–2 with fine sand. Limewash: putty thinned to milk.</li><li>Keep lime work damp and shaded for days, out of frost and hard rain for weeks.</li></ul></div></div>')
    h.append('<h2 id="pozz">3.4 Pozzolans and Roman-style concrete</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Plain lime only hardens where air reaches it. Mix lime with a <b>pozzolan</b> — a fine material rich in reactive (glassy, amorphous) silica and alumina — and it sets with water into calcium silicate hydrate, the same glue that makes portland cement strong. Romans built harbors, the Pantheon and aqueducts this way, using volcanic ash from Pozzuoli (Jackson et al., 2017; Seymour et al., 2023).</p>')
    h.append(table(["Pozzolan", "Where / how", "Notes"], [
        ["Volcanic ash, pumice, tuff", "Ash beds, pumice fields and soft tuffs across the western US, BC, Alaska, Mexico, Central America (Part 9)", "Must be glassy, not crystal/lithic; grind fine (flour-like, < 75 µm best)"],
        ["Crushed fired brick / tile (cocciopesto)", "Low-fired red brick and pottery, ground to powder", "Best from bricks fired ~600–900 °C; hard-fired brick less reactive"],
        ["Calcined clay (homemade metakaolin)", "Burn clay lumps at 600–800 °C (dull-cherry red) for a few hours, then grind", "Kaolin-rich white or pale clays react best"],
        ["Rice husk ash, and ash of high-silica plants (bamboo, horsetail, straw)", "Burn at < 700 °C in a controlled heap or drum so the ash stays gray-black and glassy", "White, crystalline overburnt ash is weak. Grind fine"],
        ["Fly ash", "Coal power-plant stockpiles", "Very good; widely used in modern concrete"],
        ["Wood ash", "Fireplace ash", "<b>Not a reliable pozzolan</b> — mostly calcium/potassium carbonates and salts. Use only as minor filler; never in reinforced work"]]))
    h.append('<p><b>Recipes</b> (by volume): mortar 1 lime putty : 2–3 pozzolan, or 1 lime : 1 pozzolan : 2 sand. Concrete: 1 lime : 2 pozzolan : 3–4 broken stone or brick, rammed into place in layers 4–6 in thick (the Romans tamped rather than poured). Keep it damp — pozzolanic concrete gains strength over weeks and months, not days. Adding even 10% portland cement, if you have any, speeds the set a lot.</p>')
    h.append(tip("Test whether an ash or clay is pozzolanic", "Make three small pats: (1) 1 lime putty : 2 candidate powder; (2) 1 lime : 2 ordinary fine sand (control); (3) candidate powder + water only. Keep all three covered and damp for 7, 14 and 28 days. If pat 1 is clearly harder than pat 2 (can’t scratch with a fingernail, rings when tapped) and holds together after a day soaking in water, you have a pozzolan."))
    h.append('<h2 id="hydraulic">3.5 Hydraulic lime, natural cement — and why not portland?</h2>')
    h.append('<p><b>Hydraulic lime</b> comes from limestone with roughly 6–20% clay impurities, burned at about 1,000–1,250 °C. Some of the lime combines with the clay into belite (dicalcium silicate), which sets with water. Slake it to a dry powder with just enough water and use it soon. Test your stone: if burnt lumps slake sluggishly and a pat of the putty hardens under water within a week or two, it is hydraulic. <b>Natural cement</b> is the stronger cousin: burnt clayey limestone (“cement rock”) that barely slakes and must be ground — 19th-century North America ran on it (Rosendale, NY; Louisville, KY; Utica, IL). It was used for the Brooklyn Bridge and Erie Canal locks.</p>')
    h.append('<p><b>True portland cement</b> needs a precise blend of limestone and clay (about 85% lime + silica, plus alumina and iron), ground very fine, burned to <b>~1,450 °C</b> (2,640–2,700 °F) until partly molten into clinker, cooled fast, then ground to flour with ~5% gypsum. That temperature is beyond wood-fired kilns without forced air and refractory lining, and the grinding needs heavy machinery. <b>Realistic at small scale</b>: salvaged cement, lime + pozzolan, hydraulic lime and natural cement from suitable stone, and soil stabilization. These are weaker and slower than portland — design thicker walls, rely on compression (arches, vaults, thick footings), and keep steel out of lime concretes (they don’t protect steel as well).</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="gypsum">3.6 Gypsum plaster</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O) is soft (fingernail scratches it), glassy-to-chalky, doesn’t fizz in acid, and occurs in beds in Michigan, Iowa, Oklahoma, Texas, New Mexico, Nevada, Utah, Nova Scotia, Ontario and northern Mexico. Heat crushed gypsum gently — <b>about 150–180 °C (300–350 °F)</b>, e.g., in a steel pan over a fire, stirring until the “boiling” (steam release) stops — and you have plaster of Paris. Mixed with water it sets in 10–30 minutes. Overheating above ~250 °C makes it set slowly. Use for interior plaster, molds, smooth wall finishes and casts. <b>Not for exterior or wet work</b> — gypsum dissolves in water. Old drywall can be crushed and re-calcined the same way (remove the paper).</p>')
    return "\n".join(h)
